1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
My midnight radio show never gets callers, until the phone rings with a voice reading my own obituary.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,348
Leo stares at the studio console as the mysterious caller hangs up, leaving only a cold dial tone behind.

3
00:00:15,648 --> 00:00:23,952
Another line lights up instantly, and a woman describes the exact floral arrangement resting at her own funeral.

4
00:00:24,252 --> 00:00:33,420
Panic grips my chest because I recognize every single detail they speak as an unprinted front-page newspaper story.

5
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:41,160
Leo tries to cut the broadcast feed, but the master switch snaps off in his bloody, trembling fingers.

6
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:50,172
The studio speakers echo a familiar voice reading tomorrow morning headlines before anyone else even knows they happened.

7
00:00:50,472 --> 00:00:58,560
A loud siren wails outside the window as flashing red lights paint the walls of the quiet broadcasting room.

8
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:07,884
Leo rushes to the glass window, watching emergency crews swarm the exact building he is currently broadcasting from.

9
00:01:08,184 --> 00:01:15,192
The studio phone rings one final time, and the voice on the other end whispers that my shift is over.

10
00:01:15,492 --> 00:01:22,644
Leo listens to the dead air, finally realizing the broadcast started long after he stopped breathing.
